    plain: `Every pod has to run on some machine in the cluster — a <b>node</b>. The <b>scheduler</b> is the part of Kubernetes that picks that machine. First it crosses off the nodes that cannot take the pod (too full, the wrong kind, or marked "keep out"), then it scores the rest and picks the best one. You can steer the choice with labels and rules. It matters because good placement keeps your app fast, and keeps one broken machine from taking every copy of your app down with it.`,
